our final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nGiven that the third term of a geometric sequence is $1053$ and the ninth term is $\\frac{13}{9}$, find the value of the seventh term.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a531ae84de890100a1e8ca9b893b21bc24d2561578b6a92c616e2b8f9a2bc940"},"seconds":18.103192567825317,"seed":539161518989818840,"stop":"eos","text":"Recognize that:\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{1}{729} = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(a\\) using \\(ar^2 = 1053\\):\n\\[\na \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na = 1053 \\times 9 = 9477\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6 = 9477 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6 = 9477 \\cdot \\frac{1}{729} = \\frac{9477}{729} = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the seventh term is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for the seventh term of the geometric sequence, we start by recalling the general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence:\n\\[\na_n = ar^{n-1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term and \\(r\\) is the common ratio.\n\nGiven:\n- The third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\)\n- The ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nUsing the general form, we can write:\n\\[\na_3 = ar^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na_9 = ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\n\\]\n\nWe now have two equations:\n1. \\(ar^2 = 1053\\)\n2. \\(ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nTo find \\(r\\), we divide the second equation by the first equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{ar^8}{ar^2} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117}\n\\]\n\nRecognize that:\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{1}{729} = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(a\\) using \\(ar^2 = 1053\\):\n\\[\na \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na = 1053 \\times 9 = 9477\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6 = 9477 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6 = 9477 \\cdot \\frac{1}{729} = \\frac{9477}{729} = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the seventh term is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]","id":"algebra:753"}
